Subject: logs: fsync() log files after writing on VMS so readers see the data

On OpenVMS, data written to a file with write() is not visible to other
readers (TYPE, SEARCH, sftp, a log shipper) until the file's end-of-file
mark is updated, which happens at close or when the file is flushed.
lighttpd keeps its error and access logs open for its whole life, so they
looked empty while the server ran (Phase 2: an empty error.log after
failed requests).  fsync() flushes the file and updates the EOF.  Call it
after each write to a log *file* (not pipes, stderr or syslog).  The error
log is written rarely; the access log is written in 8 KB batches or by the
periodic flush, so the cost is small.

--- a/src/log.c
+++ b/src/log.c
@@ -289,6 +289,9 @@
     if (errh->mode != FDLOG_SYSLOG) { /* FDLOG_FD FDLOG_FILE FDLOG_PIPE */
         buffer_append_char(b, '\n');
         write_all(errh->fd, BUF_PTR_LEN(b));
+      #ifdef __VMS
+        if (errh->mode == FDLOG_FILE) fsync(errh->fd); /* make it visible */
+      #endif
     }
     else {
       #ifdef HAVE_SYSLOG_H
--- a/src/fdlog_maint.c
+++ b/src/fdlog_maint.c
